<Doc title="The breadboard" subtitle="what is connected to what" category="Circuits" id="breadboard">

<Table>
<Row head="yes"><Cell>group</Cell><Cell>what is joined</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell>main row</Cell><Cell>five holes are one node</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell>centre channel</Cell><Cell>splits left from right; a chip straddles it so its two rows of pins stay separate</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell class="power">+ rail</Cell><Cell>TWO halves, broken in the middle</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell class="ground">- rail</Cell><Cell>TWO halves, broken in the middle</Cell></Row>
</Table>

<Draw width="640" height="320">
# 24 columns, not a realistic 30-odd. The width has to leave room for the
# annotations at the right of the page, and a breadboard drawing that runs
# under its own labels teaches nothing that a shorter one does not.
#
# Tall enough for both banks of five plus the rails: the last row sits at
# by0 + 253, so anything under 267 puts holes outside the board.
setColor grey
setWidth 1
drawRect 30 16 448 302

# ---- the power rails. NOT one long connection.
#
# On most full-size boards each rail is two separate halves with a break in
# the middle, and the break is nearly invisible - the printed stripe usually
# runs straight through it. This used to be drawn as an unbroken line, which
# is a comfortable lie and an expensive one.
setColor red
setWidth 2
drawLine 40 32 228 32
drawLine 250 32 438 32
# The break itself, drawn as the thing it is rather than as an absence.
setColor white
setWidth 1.5
drawLine 234 27 244 37
drawLine 234 37 244 27
setColor red
setWidth 1
drawCircle 44 42 3.2
drawCircle 61 42 3.2
drawCircle 78 42 3.2
drawCircle 95 42 3.2
drawCircle 112 42 3.2
drawCircle 129 42 3.2
drawCircle 146 42 3.2
drawCircle 163 42 3.2
drawCircle 180 42 3.2
